#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105489 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a87f2499-6998-587a-930e-65845c94710c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals on Which the Derivative of a Graphed Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/id #uuid "6b6ba1b8-5041-57c3-bcbb-239af0faa4dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-3722" :question/problem "The graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ is shown below. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x=1$ and $x=4$. Given that $f'(x) = 0$ at both $A$ and $B$, find the intervals where $f'(x)>0$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1359/Source/Images/188a9a9a3d8e58ae595eacd0c7a3e567.png)" :question/worked-solution "We have $f'(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B$. Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then $f'(x)>0$.\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then $f'(x)<0$.\n\nThe information about $f'(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & (- ∞, 1) & 1 & (1, 4) & 4 & (4, + ∞) \\\\ f & ↘ & min & ↗ & max & ↘ \\\\ f^{′} & - & 0 & + & 0 & -\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f'(x)>0$ on the interval $(1,4)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105491 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b466b35-cb4d-5082-9989-9c767af757f8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Decreasing Using Differentiation"}] :question/id #uuid "7be416a1-e729-58a4-bc3c-d622d058eba8" :question/math-academy-id "e-3728" :question/problem "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x) = x^3 + x^2 - 8x$ is strictly decreasing." :question/worked-solution "To find where $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, we need to solve the inequality $f'(x) < 0$.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= 3x^{2} + 2x - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n3x^{2} + 2x - 8 < 0\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n3(x + 2)\\left(x -\\dfrac 4 3\\right) < 0\n$$\n\nand the graph of\n\n$$\ny=3(x+2)\\left(x -\\dfrac 4 3\\right)\n$$\n\nhas roots at $x=-2$ and\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac 4 3\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1359/Source/Images/75d6da8209635228de08be5e9bd44737.png)\n\nThe graph is negative on the interval\n\n$$\n\\left(-2, \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nbetween the roots, so we conclude that $f'(x) < 0$ on the interval\n\n$$\n\\left(-2, \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval\n\n$$\n\\left(-2, \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105494 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1671fb4f-937f-5a3c-85b7-7f54f35ea543" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Increasing Using Differentiation"}] :question/id #uuid "cc1f0cb5-5cca-5a70-a89f-2983a64e273f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3731" :question/problem "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{\\ln{2x}}{x}$ (plotted below) is strictly increasing.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1359/Source/Images/39ade411a2a5987fa7b7c2350ac26caf.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find where $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we need to solve the inequality $f'(x) > 0$.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x)$ using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= ((\\frac{1}{x}) \\cdot x - \\ln (2x) \\cdot 1)/(x^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - \\ln 2x}{x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1-\\ln{2x}}{x^2} > 0\n$$\n\nThe denominator is always positive, so this reduces to\n\n$$\n1 - \\ln 2x > 0⟹\\ln 2x < 1\n$$\n\nSolving $\\ln{2x} = 1$ gives the critical point\n\n$$\n2x = e\\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad x = \\dfrac{1}{2}e\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that this critical point is a maximum. We also see that the slope of the tangent lines is positive to the left of the critical point and negative to the right of the critical point.\n\nWe summarize this information in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & (0, \\frac{1}{2}e) & \\frac{1}{2}e & (\\frac{1}{2}e, ∞) \\\\ f & ↗ & max & ↘ \\\\ f^{′} & + & 0 & -\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the interval\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\frac 1 2 e\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich can also be written as\n\n$$\n0 < x < \\dfrac 1 2 e\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105495 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b77217ef-9076-5365-bebb-d2b46e64c9be" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Slopes of Functions at Different Points"}] :question/id #uuid "88cdcba5-2572-5a6a-aa99-826b80cb1d46" :question/math-academy-id "e-3734" :question/problem "Consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1359/Source/Images/57462b1da853695603be9e97d89cb7a4.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements is true?\n\n1. $f'(2.5) < f'(1) < f'(-1)$\n2. $f'(-1) < f'(1) < f'(2.5)$\n3. $f'(1) < f'(-1) < f'(2.5)$" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that\n\n- the slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is positive, so $f'(-1) > 0$.\n- the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is zero, so $f'(1) = 0$.\n- the slope of the tangent at $x=2.5$ is negative, and so $f'(2.5) < 0$.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is correct."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a87f2499-6998-587a-930e-65845c94710c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals on Which the Derivative of a Graphed Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4256cc9-0a07-5c54-99b9-1a798c93de20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2, \\infty)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102328} {:answer/id #uuid "d52c0939-753a-541e-8daf-f26283cc2bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(- \\infty, 0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102329} {:answer/id #uuid "dc545ee8-7ae9-5f37-806d-c46d2033a4e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No interval exists where $f^{′}(x) < 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102330} {:answer/id #uuid "6064469b-87d0-50bf-a062-12a4fd32c094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(- \\infty, 0)$ and $(2, \\infty)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102331} {:answer/id #uuid "716b2fa2-87c6-51ae-8fc6-b92fdf71ca00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "716b2fa2-87c6-51ae-8fc6-b92fdf71ca00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102332} :answer-field/id #uuid "1454d63e-c5a6-52c5-81d9-32e1a6416d94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102327}] :question/id #uuid "4c0a1416-8923-507f-b1c1-0e9a65b61f26" :question/math-academy-id "q-18227" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1359/Source/Images/q-18227.png)\n\nThe graph of the continuous function $y = f(x)$ is shown below. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x = 0$ and $x = 2$. Given that $f^{′}(x) = 0$ at both $A$ and $B$, find the intervals where $f^{′}(x) < 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b77217ef-9076-5365-bebb-d2b46e64c9be" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Slopes of Functions at Different Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c3b1fb7-c5f4-5584-bcdd-0fadb99fe83e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(0) < f^{′}(1) < f^{′}(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104238} {:answer/id #uuid "ccda6cd1-fb01-5eaa-ad36-c61e86021991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(1) < f^{′}(0) < f^{′}(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104239} {:answer/id #uuid "29798f0a-acb2-5f3e-8c58-0507c31b6983" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(0) < f^{′}(3) < f^{′}(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104240} {:answer/id #uuid "36710fe9-febe-5e14-82be-721b4fd6e2b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(3) < f^{′}(0) < f^{′}(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104241} {:answer/id #uuid "6033e3ff-be38-59f8-98c9-f455bb8ab000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(1) < f^{′}(3) < f^{′}(0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6033e3ff-be38-59f8-98c9-f455bb8ab000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(1) < f^{′}(3) < f^{′}(0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104242} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf6956d2-b211-58bd-b5cb-d455beaf513a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104237}] :question/id #uuid "f4beddd7-ca54-5c40-8a6b-2e4b3e0488da" :question/math-academy-id "q-48996" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1359/Source/Images/q-48996.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y = f(x)$ above. Which of the following statements is true?"}]}
